            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "<h3><strong><strong>What You’ll Learn</strong></strong><br></h3><p>We move beyond surface-level aesthetics to focus on advanced visual grammar and intentional narrative controls:</p><ul><li><strong><strong>The Exposure Triangle for Narrative:</strong></strong> Using shutter speed and aperture not just for correct exposure, but as intentional storytelling tools (e.g. slow-shutter panning shots to isolate kinetic subjects in motion against static cityscapes, deliberate motion drag, and selective focal planes).</li><li><strong><strong>Rule of Thirds &amp; Dynamic Intersection:</strong></strong> Strategic placement of narrative anchors where the viewer's eye naturally travels.</li><li><strong><strong>Leading Lines &amp; Spatial Depth:</strong></strong> Directing visual momentum through urban geometry and perspective compression.</li><li><strong><strong>Dynamic Diagonals:</strong></strong> Generating tension and visual energy within static architectural compositions.</li><li><strong><strong>Natural &amp; Architectural Framing:</strong></strong> Exploiting structural cutouts, shadow pockets, and archways to isolate subjects cleanly.</li><li><strong><strong>Figure-to-Ground Contrast:</strong></strong> Spotting subtle illumination differences to make subjects pop distinctly off complex urban backdrops.</li><li><strong><strong>Intentional Proximity &amp; Filling the Frame:</strong></strong> Eliminating clutter to build immediate intimacy.</li><li><strong><strong>Center-Dominant Eye Alignment:</strong></strong> Crafting compelling, piercing portrait gazes.</li><li><strong><strong>Patterns, Repetition &amp; Rhythm Interruption:</strong></strong> Building visual cadence and capturing the exact moment an anomaly breaks it.</li><li><strong><strong>Geometric Symmetry &amp; Balance:</strong></strong> Creating powerful visual equilibrium.</li><li><strong><strong>Subverting the Rules:</strong></strong> Learning when to break these rules for maximum impact.</li></ul>",
